00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.870 align:start position:0%
thinking back to that sunny day sitting  in a coffee shop talking with my friends  about how we should be doing more to  tackle climate change  we knew we wanted to address fast  fashion but we needed more than just a  vague topic for our solidarity project  to help us develop the idea further we  used a technique called the problem and  solution tree  it helped us to better understand the  issue and its possible causes and  effects leading us to more concrete  ideas for technical topic  here is how it works  first you identify the main problem that  you want to address  for us it was the environmental impact  of the fast fashion industry  this problem is represented as the trunk  of the tree  and task goes into the middle of the  paper  the roots of our problem go into well  the roots of the tree  in our discussions we identified some of  the main causes of this issue to be  financial barriers  educational barriers and social  pressures  finally the branches are where we see  the effects of the issue  this is what the roots and the trunk  have ultimately produced for example by  not being aware of the problem the  industry thrives creating tons of  non-recyclable waste filling our oceans  with microplastics while also creating  an ever-growing need for more clothes  created from non-renewable resources  continuing the cycle  it's not exactly a pretty looking tree  is it  with the second tree the trunk now  represents the desired outcome that we  want to see  for our project we wanted consumers to  be aware of their choices and the  respective consequences  we've wanted to encourage a repair  before we replace mentality as the roots  are the pathways to the outcome  this is where we wrote down ideas of  action and activities that we could do  to reach our goal for example  organizing workshops to teach people how  to repair clothes  creating online content to provide  reliable information  or giving speeches to raise awareness  finally the branches of the solution  tree represent the impact that these  activities could lead to for example  with new sewing skills people might be  more willing to repair their clothes  before throwing them away  if people knew the reality of the  industry perhaps they would think twice  before buying three new shirts when they  only need one  it was useful to explore these trees as  a team because each new idea on the  problem tree gave us new ideas for the  actions and impacts we wanted to see in  our solution tree once the trees were  finished we show them to our friends and  family to see if the problems in the  solution match their realities or if  there were roots and branches that we  had overlooked  this is something that helped us and we  think it will help you too  [Music]  you
 

